---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import ExplanationEntry from "../../../components/story/ExplanationEntry.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, computed, crucial, composition, kansasCruz, kansasPoll, groups, countyTypes, stateSwings } from "../../../data/midterms-2026-2026-11-03-why-voters-turned-on-republicans.mjs";
const story = "midterms-2026--2026-11-03-why-voters-turned-on-republicans";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short;
const pts = (m) => (m > 0 ? `R+${m.toFixed(1)}` : m < 0 ? `D+${(-m).toFixed(1)}` : "Even");
const warStory = "/events/iran-war/2026-09-15-cbo-estimates-38-billion-war-cost/";
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/midterms-2026/">2026 midterms</a><span>November 3, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">2026 midterms &middot; November 3, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="dems-win-both-chambers" /><Cite c="crucial-corruption" /><Cite c="crucial-cost" /><Cite c="crucial-iran" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-moved">Who changed sides, and who stayed home</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Gas, prices and an unpopular president</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">What each side says lost it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every explanation, tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Democrats won control of the House and the Senate on Nov. 3. By Nov. 10 they had won {n.demHouse} of the 435 House seats, with some races still uncalled.<Cite c="dems-win-both-chambers" /> In the Senate, Democrats flipped seven Republican seats, from Texas and Kansas to Maine, and were on track for {n.demSenate}.<Cite c="senate-flips" /><Cite c="dems-win-both-chambers" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Voter Poll, run by the research firm SSRS for seven news organizations, asked voters what drove their choice; its national file has {n.respondents} respondents.<Cite c="one-poll-seven-sponsors" /><Cite c="poll-size" /> Each publishes it under its own name, so the NBC News Exit Poll, the CBS News exit poll, the Fox News Voter Poll and the AP Voter Poll are the same survey.<Cite c="one-poll-seven-sponsors" /> It asked voters whether each of five issues was crucial to their vote for Congress. More said so of government corruption than of anything else, and the war in Iran came fourth.<Cite c="issues-asked-separately" /><Cite c="crucial-corruption" /><Cite c="crucial-cost" /><Cite c="crucial-immigration" /><Cite c="crucial-iran" /><Cite c="crucial-ai" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Share of voters who called each issue crucial to their vote for Congress</strong></figcaption>
          <ul class="share-bars" aria-label="Share of voters calling each issue crucial: corruption, cost of living, immigration enforcement, the Iran war, AI and data centers">
            {crucial.map((r) => (
              <li>
                <span class="share-label">{short(r.c)}<Cite c={r.c} /></span>
                <span class="share-track"><span class="share-bar" style={`width:${r.value}%`}></span></span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="chart-source">The Voter Poll by SSRS, national House sample, {n.respondents} voters. Each issue was asked about on its own, so a voter could call several crucial.<Cite c="poll-size" /><Cite c="issues-asked-separately" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Trump was not on the ballot, but {n.oppose}% of voters said one reason for their House vote was to oppose him, against {n.support}% who voted to support him; {n.notFactor}% said he was not a factor.<Cite c="trump-factor" /> {n.approve}% approved of the job he is doing.<Cite c="trump-approval" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="who-moved">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who changed sides, and who stayed home</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">By their own account, more of Trump's 2024 voters stayed home than switched sides. An Economist/YouGov poll of 3,166 registered voters, taken after the election, also asked people who did not vote.<Cite c="yougov-trump-voters" /> In it, {n.ygTrumpHome}% of Trump's 2024 voters said they did not vote, and {n.ygTrumpD}% said they voted for a Democrat for the House.<Cite c="yougov-turnout-2024" /><Cite c="yougov-trump-voters" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>What 2024 voters did in 2026, by their own account</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>2024 vote</th><th class="num">Voted Republican</th><th class="num">Voted Democratic</th><th class="num">Did not vote</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Trump</td><td class="num">{n.ygTrumpR}%</td><td class="num">{n.ygTrumpD}%</td><td class="num">{n.ygTrumpHome}%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Harris</td><td class="num">{n.ygHarrisR}%</td><td class="num">{n.ygHarrisD}%</td><td class="num">{n.ygHarrisHome}%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="chart-source">Economist/YouGov poll of 3,166 registered voters, Nov. 4 to 6; House vote. Rows do not add to 100 because some voted for other candidates.<Cite c="yougov-trump-voters" /><Cite c="yougov-turnout-2024" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Voter Poll surveys people who voted.<Cite c="poll-size" /> In it, {n.trumpToDem}% of voters who said they backed Trump in 2024 voted for a Democrat for the House, and {n.harrisToRep}% of Harris voters went the other way.<Cite c="trump-voters-switched" /> Voters who said they backed Harris made up {n.harris2024}% of this year's voters and Trump's voters {n.trump2024}%, though Trump won the 2024 popular vote, {n.trump2024Pop}% to {n.harris2024Pop}%.<Cite c="vote-2024-composition" /><Cite c="trump-won-2024-popular" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How this year's voters said they voted in 2024</strong></figcaption>
          <ul class="share-bars" aria-label="2026 voters by their 2024 vote: Harris, Trump, did not vote">
            {composition.map((r) => (
              <li>
                <span class="share-label">{r.label}: {r.value}%<Cite c="vote-2024-composition" /></span>
                <span class="share-track"><span class="share-bar" style={`width:${r.value}%`}></span></span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="chart-source">The Voter Poll by SSRS. The 2024 vote is what voters recalled, and the poll's weights use it.<Cite c="poll-weighted-to-vote" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A second survey of more than 90,000 voters, by the research firm NORC with The Washington Post and other sponsors, found about 1 in 10 Trump voters backing the Democrat for Senate in Iowa, Kansas, North Carolina and Texas.<Cite c="norc-switch-and-new" /> The two surveys disagree on how many of this year's voters had not voted in 2024: {n.nonvoters2024}% in the Voter Poll, about {n.norcNew}% in NORC's.<Cite c="nonvoter-share-differs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Reports of how far a group moved since 2024 differ partly because the Voter Poll began in 2025 and has no 2024 edition, so each outlet set it against a different 2024 survey. For Latino voters' 2024 support for Trump, NBC used 46% and AP roughly 4 in 10.<Cite c="poll-is-new" /><Cite c="baseline-latino-nbc" /><Cite c="baseline-latino-ap" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">How each group voted</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Democrats won men narrowly and women by 19 points. They won every age group except voters 65 and older, who split evenly, and every income group. White voters split evenly; Republicans won voters without a college degree and those in small towns and rural areas.<Cite c="groups-detail" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>2026 House vote by group</strong></figcaption>
          <p class="party-key" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pk d">Democratic</span><span class="pk r">Republican</span></p>
          {groups.map((g) => (
            <div class="group-set">
              <p class="group-name">{g.set}</p>
              <ul class="split-bars">
                {g.rows.map(([label, d, r]) => (
                  <li aria-label={`${label}: Democratic ${d}%, Republican ${r}%`}>
                    <span class="split-label">{label}</span>
                    <span class="split-track"><span class="split-d" style={`width:${d}%`}>{d}</span><span class="split-r" style={`width:${r}%`}>{r}</span></span>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
          <p class="chart-source">The Voter Poll by SSRS, national House sample, {n.respondents} voters. Shares of each group's vote; the poll has no 2024 edition to compare with.<Cite c="groups-detail" /><Cite c="poll-is-new" /></p>
        </figure>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Where the vote moved</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In the counties The Washington Post tracked, in returns as of 8:30 a.m. Nov. 4, the Senate vote moved furthest toward Democrats, against the 2024 presidential vote, in majority-Hispanic counties, by 28 points, and least in small cities and rural areas, by about 16.<Cite c="wapo-county-types" /> By state, Texas moved most, about 26 points, then Rhode Island, North Carolina, Mississippi and South Carolina, 22 to 23. Minnesota, South Dakota and Michigan moved least, about 6 to 8 points.<Cite c="wapo-state-swings" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Republican margin in the same counties: 2024 presidential vote against the 2026 Senate vote</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Kind of county</th><th class="num">Trump 2024</th><th class="num">GOP Senate 2026</th><th class="num">Change</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{countyTypes.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="num">{pts(r.m24)}</td><td class="num">{pts(r.m26)}</td><td class="num">{(r.m26 - r.m24).toFixed(1)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="chart-source">Counties in states with a Senate race; margins in points, Republican minus Democratic. The Washington Post, from Associated Press counts as of 8:30 a.m. Nov. 4, before every vote was in. A Senate race is not the same contest as the presidential race.<Cite c="wapo-county-types" /></p>
        </figure>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far each state's Senate vote moved toward Democrats from the 2024 presidential vote, in points</strong></figcaption>
          <ul class="swing-bars" aria-label="Shift toward Democrats by state, largest first">
            {stateSwings.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <span class="swing-label">{s.name} <span class="swing-region">{s.region}</span></span>
                <span class="swing-track"><span class="swing-bar" style={`width:${(-s.swing / 30) * 100}%`}></span><span class="swing-num">{Math.round(-s.swing)}</span></span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="chart-source">Places, not voters. The Post's county returns summed by state: the 2026 Senate margin against the 2024 presidential margin in the same counties. Associated Press counts as of 8:30 a.m. Nov. 4, before every vote was in. Colorado, Maine, Massachusetts, New Hampshire and Oregon are left out because few of their counties had reported.<Cite c="wapo-state-swings" /> Later official counts in Texas, South Carolina and North Carolina give the same shifts to within a tenth of a point.<Cite c="later-counts-match" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Gas, prices and an unpopular president</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The U.S. and Israel <a href={warStory}>went to war with Iran</a> in February.<Cite c="war-began-february" /> The national average price of regular gasoline was ${n.gasFeb} a gallon in the week of Feb. 23 and ${n.gasNov} in the week of Nov. 2.<Cite c="gas-prices" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In the Voter Poll, {n.hardship}% said inflation had caused their family hardship in the past year, and they voted Democratic 62% to 36%.<Cite c="inflation-hardship" /> Asked why prices were high, {n.pricesTrump}% said it was more Trump's policies than things outside his control, and {n.tariffsNational}% said his tariffs had hurt the national economy.<Cite c="prices-trump-policies" /><Cite c="tariffs-hurt" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In late August, the polling firm Gallup measured Trump's approval at 33%, the lowest reading before a midterm since Harry Truman's in 1946.<Cite c="trump-approval-gallup-aug" /> Among the people who voted, the Voter Poll put it at {n.approve}% and NORC at {n.norcApprove}%.<Cite c="trump-approval" /><Cite c="norc-approval" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What each side says lost it</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Vice President JD Vance blamed the war, in a post on Nov. 4, and said the problem was 2024 Trump voters who switched, not voters who stayed home.<Cite c="vance-war" /> He said Trump agreed. On Fox News the night before, Trump had told the anchor Bret Baier it was the gasoline price, not the war.<Cite c="trump-gas-price" /><Cite c="trump-part-iran" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>What Vance said Trump said, and what Trump said</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="side-by-side quotes">
            <blockquote class="said"><p class="said-who">JD Vance &middot; Nov. 4, 2026 &middot; post on X</p><p>"The reason we lost is the war. The president himself said so last night."<Cite c="vance-war" /></p></blockquote>
            <blockquote class="said"><p class="said-who">Donald Trump &middot; Nov. 3, 2026 &middot; Fox News, election night</p><p>"I don't think it's the war, Bret, I think it's the gasoline price."<Cite c="trump-gas-price" /></p><p>"I think a part of it was Iran."<Cite c="trump-part-iran" /></p></blockquote>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On Nov. 4 Trump called Vance's post "largely true because it drove the gasoline up a little bit short term," meaning the war had raised gas prices.<Cite c="trump-largely-true" /> In an interview on CBS's 60 Minutes aired Nov. 8, he said of the loss, "it's not really the Iran war," and "I think it was bad messaging." He said his deportation policy "might be too tough."<Cite c="trump-60min-messaging" /><Cite c="trump-60min-immigration" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Vance's post also gave two numbers. He wrote that Florida's electorate was 9 points more Republican than Democratic. In Florida's Voter Poll, 41% of voters called themselves Republicans and 30% Democrats, which roughly fits.<Cite c="vance-florida" /><Cite c="florida-poll" /> The Voter Poll has no 2024 edition, so his comparison, "just a couple points worse than 2024," cannot be checked against it.<Cite c="poll-is-new" /> He wrote that a pollster he did not name expected Republicans to lose the generic ballot, the national vote for each party's House candidates, by 13 points; the national House vote was about 12 points Democratic on Nov. 5 and expected to reach 13.<Cite c="vance-generic-13" /><Cite c="pew-house-13" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Sen. Ted Cruz of Texas blamed both prices and policy: "Tariffs have made everything too expensive, and the Iran war has dragged on too long."<Cite c="cruz-prices-tariffs" /> Peter Navarro, a White House adviser, said the tariffs "have played no role in the inflation and affordability issue."<Cite c="navarro-no-role" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Democratic leaders said voters chose them on prices and corruption. Rep. Hakeem Jeffries, the House Democratic leader, said, "We ran on affordability. We won on affordability."<Cite c="jeffries-affordability" /> Sen. Chuck Schumer said Republicans had ignored voters' concerns about "costs, chaos, and corruption."<Cite c="schumer-costs-corruption" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>USA Today printed a Kansas issue list that the Kansas exit poll does not contain<Cite c="usatoday-kansas" /><Cite c="kansas-poll-top-issue" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">On Nov. 5, USA Today wrote that in Kansas, which had elected its first Democratic senator since 1932, "nearly one-third of voters listed the cost of living as their No. 1 concern," followed by corruption, the Iran war, terrible candidates and the trade war with Canada.<Cite c="usatoday-kansas" /> The list is Cruz's. He posted it on Nov. 4 as what exit polling showed, to one decimal place: 30.6% for the cost of living, 7.9% for "The candidates are terrible," 7.6% for "The Canada trade war." He did not name a poll.<Cite c="cruz-kansas-list" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The exit poll in Kansas is the Voter Poll. It asked {n.ksRespondents} Kansas voters a different question from the national "crucial" list: to pick the most important issue facing the country from six choices. The state of democracy came first and the economy second. None of Cruz's choices was on its list.<Cite c="kansas-poll-top-issue" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>Two lists of Kansas voters' top issue</strong></figcaption>
                <div class="side-by-side">
                  <table class="data-table">
                    <thead><tr><th colspan="2">Cruz's "exit polling," repeated by USA Today<Cite c="cruz-kansas-list" /></th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>{kansasCruz.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td>{k}</td><td class="num">{v}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                  </table>
                  <table class="data-table">
                    <thead><tr><th colspan="2">The Kansas Voter Poll<Cite c="kansas-poll-top-issue" /></th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>{kansasPoll.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td>{k}</td><td class="num">{v}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </figure>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every explanation, tested</h2>
        <ul class="ex-legend" data-layer="fact" aria-label="What the verdicts mean">
          <li><span class="ex-key supported">Supported</span> the voter surveys back it</li>
          <li><span class="ex-key partly">Partly supported</span> some of the evidence backs it and some cuts against it</li>
          <li><span class="ex-key not">Not supported</span> the evidence cuts against it</li>
          <li><span class="ex-key untestable">Can't be tested</span> nothing public measures it</li>
        </ul>

        <ExplanationEntry name="The Iran war" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Vance says the war cost Republicans the election.<Cite c="vance-war" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">{n.iranWrong}% of voters said attacking Iran was the wrong decision, and they voted Democratic 83% to 15%.<Cite c="iran-wrong-decision" /> In NORC's survey, more than a third named the war among the top three national issues, and over 6 in 10 of them backed Democrats for the House.<Cite c="norc-iran-third" /> In an online poll of 1,006 midterm voters by Narrative Strategies, which did not use a random sample, {n.nsIran}% named the war among the two things most responsible for rising costs, more than named tariffs or fuel prices.<Cite c="ns-rising-costs" /> In a YouGov survey of 1,000 adults for the Institute for Global Affairs, {n.igaPrices}% said avoiding higher prices at home should come before the war's goals.<Cite c="iga-prices-over-war" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">{n.iran}% called the war crucial to their vote, fewer than said so of corruption, the cost of living or immigration enforcement.<Cite c="crucial-iran" /><Cite c="crucial-corruption" /><Cite c="crucial-cost" /><Cite c="crucial-immigration" /> Asked in their own words by the Economist/YouGov poll what mattered most, {n.ygWar}% named foreign policy and war.<Cite c="yougov-most-important" /> By Vance's account, the unnamed pollster he cites asked defecting Trump voters what would bring them back, not why they left.<Cite c="vance-pollster-question" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">A third of voters called the war crucial, but in their own words only {n.ygWar}% named it as what mattered most; many blamed it for rising prices, and Trump agreed it "drove the gasoline up." The polls cannot separate the war from the prices it helped raise.<Cite c="crucial-iran" /><Cite c="yougov-most-important" /><Cite c="ns-rising-costs" /><Cite c="trump-largely-true" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Gas and prices" verdict="supported">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Trump says voters were reacting to the price of gasoline; Cruz says food, gas and everything else cost too much.<Cite c="trump-gas-price" /><Cite c="cruz-prices-tariffs" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">{n.costMajor}% called the cost of living crucial or a major factor.<Cite c="crucial-cost" /> 87% were concerned about the cost of gas, and the 59% who were very concerned voted Democratic 66% to 32%.<Cite c="gas-concern" /> The {n.hardship}% who said inflation hurt their family voted Democratic 62% to 36%, and the {n.worse}% who said their finances were worse than two years ago, 75% to 23%.<Cite c="inflation-hardship" /><Cite c="finances-worse" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Prices weighed on nearly nine in ten voters, and those hurt by inflation went Democratic. {n.pricesTrump}% said prices were high more because of Trump's policies than things outside his control, so this explanation overlaps with a vote on Trump himself.<Cite c="crucial-cost" /><Cite c="inflation-hardship" /><Cite c="prices-trump-policies" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Tariffs" verdict="supported">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Cruz says tariffs made everything too expensive, and Phillip Magness, writing in The Dispatch, argued that tariffs, not the war, sank Republicans.<Cite c="cruz-prices-tariffs" /><Cite c="magness-not-war" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">The Supreme Court struck down Trump's emergency tariffs on Feb. 20; on July 23 he imposed new ones of 10% or 12.5% on 60 economies under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974.<Cite c="tariff-sequence" /> {n.tariffsNational}% of voters said his tariffs hurt the national economy, and they voted Democratic 81% to 18%.<Cite c="tariffs-hurt" /> In Narrative Strategies' online poll, {n.nsTariffs}% named tariffs among the top causes of rising costs.<Cite c="ns-rising-costs" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Navarro, the White House adviser, says tariffs "have played no role in the inflation and affordability issue."<Cite c="navarro-no-role" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Two-thirds of voters judged the tariffs harmful and voted against the party that imposed them. The poll measures that judgment, not what the tariffs did to prices.<Cite c="tariffs-hurt" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Affordability and corruption" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Jeffries and Schumer say voters chose Democrats on the cost of living and corruption.<Cite c="jeffries-affordability" /><Cite c="schumer-costs-corruption" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">Corruption ({n.corruption}%) and the cost of living ({n.costOfLiving}%) were the two issues the most voters called crucial.<Cite c="crucial-corruption" /><Cite c="crucial-cost" /> {n.ownInterests}% said Trump puts his own interests ahead of the country's.<Cite c="trump-own-interests" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Voters preferred Democrats only narrowly to handle them: 44% to 37% on the economy and 37% to 30% on corruption, with 32% choosing neither party on corruption.<Cite c="dem-edge-narrow" /> 52% viewed the Democratic Party unfavorably.<Cite c="dem-party-unfavorable" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Corruption and the cost of living led the crucial list, but the Democrats' edge on them was single digits, and 19% of those who viewed the party unfavorably voted for its candidates anyway.<Cite c="crucial-corruption" /><Cite c="crucial-cost" /><Cite c="dem-edge-narrow" /><Cite c="dem-party-unfavorable" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="A vote on Trump himself" verdict="supported">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">CBS called the election "clearly a referendum on Mr. Trump." Trump says he was not on the ballot, and that if he had been, his voters "would have gotten out."<Cite c="cbs-referendum" /><Cite c="trump-ballot" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">{n.oppose}% voted in part to oppose Trump and {n.support}% to support him. The {n.disapprove}% who disapproved of his job voted Democratic 86% to 12%.<Cite c="trump-factor" /><Cite c="trump-approval" /> Asked in their own words, {n.ygOpposeTrump}% of YouGov's voters named opposition to Trump, the second most common answer.<Cite c="yougov-most-important" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">{n.notFactor}% said Trump was not a factor in their vote.<Cite c="trump-factor" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">For {n.oppose + n.support}% of voters, Trump was a factor.<Cite c="trump-factor" /> Disapproving of Trump (63%) and blaming his policies for high prices (also 63%) were two separate questions, and the published tables do not show how many voters gave both answers, so a vote on Trump and a vote on prices cannot be told apart.<Cite c="trump-approval" /><Cite c="prices-trump-policies" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Bad candidates" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Trump says some voters did not like the Republican candidates, and that some he endorsed had fought him.<Cite c="trump-gas-price" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">67% of Texas Senate voters said Ken Paxton lacked the right personal character, and 55% of Kansas voters said the same of Sen. Roger Marshall. Both lost.<Cite c="paxton-character" /><Cite c="marshall-character" /><Cite c="paxton-marshall-lost" /> {n.ygCandidates}% of YouGov's voters named the candidates' qualities as what mattered most, and {n.rallies}% of registered voters said Trump's rallies hurt the Republicans he campaigned for.<Cite c="yougov-most-important" /><Cite c="yougov-rallies" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Democrats led the national House vote, across all 435 races, by about 12 points as of Nov. 5.<Cite c="house-vote-d12" /><Cite c="gop-219-going-in" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Doubts about candidates fit the Senate losses in Texas and Kansas, but not a national House vote that Democrats led by about 12 points across all 435 races.<Cite c="paxton-character" /><Cite c="house-vote-d12" /><Cite c="gop-219-going-in" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Switching, or staying home" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Vance says Republicans lost because 2024 Trump voters switched sides. A Brookings Institution analysis says discontented Republicans "didn't abandon their party in 2026; they stayed home."<Cite c="vance-war" /><Cite c="brookings-stayed-home" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">Switching: {n.trumpToDem}% of Trump's 2024 voters voted Democratic in the Voter Poll, {n.ygTrumpD}% in YouGov's.<Cite c="trump-voters-switched" /><Cite c="yougov-trump-voters" /> Staying home: among non-MAGA Republicans, {n.ygNonMagaHome}% did not vote and {n.ygNonMagaD}% voted Democratic.<Cite c="yougov-party-loyalty" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">From actual returns in 13 states, Lenny Bronner, a data scientist formerly with The Washington Post, estimated that about 75% of Democratic gains came from a turnout gap and about 20% from Republicans switching, though in Texas 45% came from switching.<Cite c="bronner-turnout" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">The surveys are what people said about their own votes, and Bronner's estimate rests on partial results. The Voter Poll and NORC disagree on how many of this year's voters skipped 2024, {n.nonvoters2024}% against about {n.norcNew}%.<Cite c="nonvoter-share-differs" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Both happened, but staying home was the larger part: {n.ygTrumpHome}% of Trump's 2024 voters said they did not vote and {n.ygTrumpD}% switched, and an early estimate from returns in 13 states puts the turnout gap ahead of switching.<Cite c="yougov-turnout-2024" /><Cite c="yougov-trump-voters" /><Cite c="bronner-turnout" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Party loyalty" verdict="supported">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Most voters vote with their party, and this time Republican voters were less loyal than Democratic ones. Asked in their own words what mattered most to their vote, more of YouGov's voters named party loyalty ({n.ygLoyalty}%) than anything else.<Cite c="yougov-most-important" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">{n.ygDemLoyal}% of Democrats said they voted for Democratic candidates, against {n.ygRepLoyal}% of Republicans for Republican ones.<Cite c="yougov-party-loyalty" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Most votes did not move, and Democrats stayed with their party more often than Republicans did.<Cite c="yougov-party-loyalty" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Redistricting and the voting-rights ruling" verdict="not">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">USA Today reported that "exit polling" showed the Supreme Court's ruling in Louisiana v. Callais on April 29, which narrowed challenges to maps under the Voting Rights Act, and the Republican redistricting that followed, were major factors for Black voters. The exit surveys it describes were the National Coalition on Black Civic Participation's own, at Southern polling places.<Cite c="callais-date" /><Cite c="usatoday-black-exit" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Roll Call found Republicans' redistricting edge "largely failed to materialize": Democrats held four of the five Texas seats Republicans targeted and all of Florida's, while Republicans won two redrawn seats, Texas's 32nd and Tennessee's 9th. Democrats also flipped seven Senate seats, in statewide races with no districts.<Cite c="redistricting-results" /><Cite c="senate-flips" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">The new lines mostly failed to deliver the gains Republicans drew them for, and cannot explain the Senate flips.<Cite c="redistricting-results" /><Cite c="senate-flips" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="The usual midterm swing" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">The Washington Post noted that midterms almost always swing against the party of the sitting president.<Cite c="wapo-midterm-swing" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">The president's party lost House seats in 20 of the 23 midterms from 1934 to 2022.<Cite c="midterm-history" /> Republicans held {n.repGoingIn} House seats going in; with Democrats at {n.demHouse} by Nov. 10, they hold at most {435 - n.demHouse}, a loss of at least {computed.repLossAtLeast}, against 41 in Trump's first midterm, in 2018.<Cite c="gop-219-going-in" /><Cite c="dems-win-both-chambers" /><Cite c="gop-loss-at-least-39" /><Cite c="trump-2018-loss" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">It is a large loss by that measure: nine of those 23 midterms cost the president's party more than {computed.repLossAtLeast} seats, among them Truman's 56 in 1946 and Obama's 63 in 2010, when his approval was about 45%.<Cite c="midterm-history" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">The Council on Foreign Relations puts the Democrats' estimated 13-point popular-vote margin second among midterms since 1942, behind only 1974.<Cite c="cfr-second-largest" /> The loss went the usual direction and is among the larger ones, under a president whose approval before the vote was the lowest since Truman's.<Cite c="midterm-history" /><Cite c="trump-approval-gallup-aug" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Democracy and presidential power" verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">A third of voters named the state of democracy as the country's most important issue, second to the economy.<Cite c="top-issue" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">Those voters chose Democrats 79% to 20%.<Cite c="democracy-voters-dem" /> 71% of all voters said Congress was not doing enough to check the president, and 24% of them still voted Republican.<Cite c="checks-not-enough" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">CBS found it was voters backing Democrats who put democracy first.<Cite c="democracy-voters-dem" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">The published tables do not show how the voters who put democracy first voted in 2024, so they cannot show whether the issue moved anyone.<Cite c="democracy-voters-dem" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Immigration raids gone too far" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">NPR wrote that Latino voters turned left after Trump's promises of lower prices never came true and his administration's harsh deportation tactics backfired.<Cite c="npr-latino-reason" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">58% said the administration's immigration actions had gone too far, and they voted Democratic 90% to 8%.<Cite c="immigration-too-far" /> Trump said on Nov. 8 that his deportation policy "might be too tough."<Cite c="trump-60min-immigration" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">The {n.immigration}% who called immigration enforcement crucial voted Republican 57% to 42%, and voters still slightly preferred Republicans on immigration, 43% to 41%.<Cite c="crucial-immigration" /><Cite c="immigration-too-far" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Immigration helped Democrats with voters who thought the raids went too far and helped Republicans with voters who put enforcement first.<Cite c="immigration-too-far" /><Cite c="crucial-immigration" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Health care costs" verdict="partly">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Schumer says Americans were clear about wanting to lower health care costs.<Cite c="schumer-health" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">After the enhanced Affordable Care Act tax credits expired at the end of 2025, what Marketplace enrollees paid in premiums rose {n.acaPremiums}% on average, KFF found.<Cite c="aca-shock" /> 55% of voters said Trump's policies had made their health care costs worse, and they voted Democratic 83% to 15%. Voters preferred Democrats on health care 50% to 30%.<Cite c="health-costs" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">8% named health care as the country's most important issue.<Cite c="top-issue" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Voters preferred Democrats on health care by 20 points, and few named it first.<Cite c="health-costs" /><Cite c="top-issue" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Abortion rights" verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Four states voted on abortion measures. KFF, which polled them, wrote that Missouri's result fit recent elections in which abortion motivated Democrats to turn out more than Republicans.<Cite c="kff-abortion-turnout" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">Voters protected abortion access in Nevada, Virginia and Missouri, and in all four states at least three in ten said the measure had a major impact on their decision to vote.<Cite c="abortion-measures" /> Nationally, the 64% who said abortion should be legal voted Democratic 78% to 21%.<Cite c="abortion-legal" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Idaho rejected its measure. In Virginia, opponents of abortion access were more likely than supporters to say the measure drove them to vote, and in Nevada 44% of voters who backed the Republican for governor also backed abortion rights.<Cite c="abortion-measures" /><Cite c="abortion-limits" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Abortion drew voters in the four states with measures, on both sides, but no public table shows it changing anyone's vote for Congress, so it cannot be tested as a cause of the national swing.<Cite c="abortion-measures" /><Cite c="abortion-limits" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="A weakening job market" verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">In NORC's survey, 58% of likely voters named "the economy and jobs" among the three most important issues facing the country.<Cite c="norc-economy-jobs" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">NORC asked about the economy and jobs as one issue, and the Voter Poll's list of top issues had the economy but no choice for jobs.<Cite c="norc-economy-jobs" /><Cite c="top-issue" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Bad messaging" verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Trump said on Nov. 8 "it's not really the Iran war" and "I think it was bad messaging."<Cite c="trump-60min-messaging" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">The voter surveys measured how voters judged conditions, such as the {n.badEconomy}% who rated the economy not so good or poor, not whether they knew the administration's record, so a message that failed cannot be told apart from conditions voters disliked.<Cite c="economy-not-good" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="Support for Israel" verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Vance wrote that defecting Trump voters told a pollster he cites that changing the relationship with Israel would help bring them back.<Cite c="vance-pollster-answers" /></p>
          <p slot="for" data-layer="narrative">{n.israelTooStrong}% of voters called U.S. support for Israel too strong.<Cite c="israel-support" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Those voters chose Democrats 79% to 19%, and the published tables do not show how many of them voted for Trump in 2024.<Cite c="israel-support" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Half of voters wanted less support for Israel, mostly voters who chose Democrats; nothing public ties the view to switching.<Cite c="israel-support" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name={'"Put America First"'} verdict="untestable">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Vance wrote that "Put America First" was another answer a pollster he cites heard from defecting Trump voters.<Cite c="vance-pollster-answers" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Vance's post lists it without a share of voters.<Cite c="vance-pollster-answers" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>

        <ExplanationEntry name="A rigged count" verdict="not">
          <p slot="claim" data-layer="narrative">Some Trump allies, among them Katie Miller and Laura Loomer, posted that the election was being stolen.<Cite c="rigged-claims" /> On Nov. 9 Trump posted, "Does anybody really believe that this 'Election' was honest," and blamed voting machines.<Cite c="trump-nov9-doubts" /></p>
          <p slot="against" data-layer="narrative">Trump said after the election that he would accept the results, there was no day-after wave of court challenges, and the FBI called one viral fraud claim false.<Cite c="rigged-claims" /> Election officials have raised no major concerns about early votes or voting machines.<Cite c="trump-nov9-doubts" /></p>
          <p slot="verdict" data-layer="narrative">Trump accepted the result before he questioned it, and election officials have raised no major concerns about early votes or voting machines.<Cite c="trump-nov9-doubts" /><Cite c="rigged-claims" /></p>
        </ExplanationEntry>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The new Congress is sworn in on Jan. 3. Democrats will need Republican votes to reach 60 in the Senate for most bills, and their majorities fall short of the two-thirds needed to override a veto.<Cite c="congress-limits" /> In a Reuters/Ipsos poll completed Nov. 9, {n.endWar}% of Americans wanted Congress to pass a law ending the war with Iran and {n.repealTariffs}% supported repealing Trump's tariffs.<Cite c="reuters-ipsos" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House has already warned against investigations. In a letter dated Nov. 9, its counsel, Will Scharf, told Rep. Robert Garcia, the top Democrat on the House Oversight Committee, that planned probes could amount to criminality; Garcia said Democrats would not be intimidated.<Cite c="oversight-letter" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Senate races in Alaska and Mississippi and more than a dozen House races were still too early to call on Nov. 10.<Cite c="dems-win-both-chambers" /> How much of the swing was switching and how much was who turned out waits on voting records: Pew says panel surveys and turnout data will answer it, and gives no date.<Cite c="pew-turnout-data" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

